{"id":1850,"date":"2020-12-08T20:20:59","date_gmt":"2020-12-09T04:20:59","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1850"},"modified":"2024-09-01T15:38:59","modified_gmt":"2024-09-01T22:38:59","slug":"codrone-python-variables","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-python-variables\/","title":{"rendered":"Variables"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215816aa9e\" name=\"6a2215816aa9e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What are variables?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Variables are important in all programming languages and in most programs! Just like in math, variables in programming are placeholders and data storage for numbers. Typically, you can create, or <strong>declare<\/strong>, a variable and save, or <strong>initialize<\/strong>, its value at the beginning of your program. You can then <strong>call <\/strong>that value later on in the program whenever you use the variable.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">x = 2\t        # Declaring variable x and initializing the value to 2\r\nprint(x)\t# Calling the variable in a print statement\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>You can declare multiple variables to hold many values. Just be sure to give them different names, because a variable can change values when running a program. If you make a new variable with the same name as a previous one, the new value will override the old value.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">x = 2\t # Declaring a variable x and initializing it to 2\r\nprint(x) # Printing initial value of x\r\nx = 3\t # Reassigning variable x to 3\r\nprint(x) # Printing new value of x\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Just like in your math class, you can do math with your variables. What do you think the following code will output? Try it out!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">y = 4\r\nz = 3\r\nx = y + z\r\nprint(x)\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>This also works with subtraction, division, multiplication and other mathematical functions! In a future lesson, you will learn about exceptions when working with operations.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215816ac42\" name=\"6a2215816ac42\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Types of variables<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You can also assign variables to other types of data, like <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">int, float, string,<\/code>and<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">boolean<\/code>.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">w = 4\t                # this is an int\r\nx = 3.2\t                # this is a float\r\nphrase = \"Hello\"\t# this is a string\r\nis_flying = True\t# this is a boolean\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Int: a negative or positive whole number<\/li>\n<li>Float: decimal values<\/li>\n<li>String: a sequence of characters<\/li>\n<li>Boolean: can be set to <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">True<\/code> or<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">False<\/code><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>If you are familiar with other programming languages, you may be wondering why we&#8217;re not including a data type in the initialization, such as:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\"># Warning! This is NOT proper Python syntax\r\nint w = 4\r\nfloat x = 3.2\r\nString phrase = \"Hello\"\r\nbool is_flying = True\r\n\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>In Python, variables can hold whatever type of value you want without needing to specify the type of data.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215816ad2c\" name=\"6a2215816ad2c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Printing variable practice<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Create four variables: one integer, one decimal, one string, and one that&#8217;s your choice. Add print statements for each variable. Run the code!<\/p>\n<p>Here&#8217;s an example:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">x = 2\r\ny = 25.3\r\nz = 0.375\r\nphrase = \"message received\"\r\n\r\nprint(x)\r\nprint(y)\r\nprint(z)\r\nprint(phrase)\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215816adf0\" name=\"6a2215816adf0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Variables and CoDrone<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Now that you know how to create variables, let\u2019s use them in a program with CoDrone! Create a variable called <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">duration<\/code>and assign it to a value that will represent the number of seconds that you want the drone to perform an action. Next, call that variable inside functions in your program.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">duration = 3.5\t        # initialize variable 'duration' to 3.5\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.hover(duration)\t# in this case, hover for 3.5 seconds\r\ndrone.set_pitch(50)\t# Set positive pitch to 50% power\r\ndrone.move(duration)\t# forward for 3.5 seconds\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215816ae6b\" name=\"6a2215816ae6b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Reassign variables<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Change the value of duration to see how it affects the CoDrone. As duration increases, the CoDrone should hover and fly longer in the air.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">duration = 5\t        # initialize variable 'duration' to 5\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.hover(duration)\t# in this case, hover for 5 seconds\r\ndrone.set_pitch(50)\t# Set positive pitch to 50% power\r\ndrone.move(duration)\t# forward for 5 seconds\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215816aeed\" name=\"6a2215816aeed\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<strong>Challenge: Variable square<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Use the setup code provided to have your CoDrone take off and then fly in a square pattern! Note: this code will only have your drone take off, throttle up, and then land. You need to do the extra work to fly your CoDrone in a square!<\/p>\n<p><strong>Rules:<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>You are only allowed to use the existing variables, but you can change the<\/li>\n<li>You can&#8217;t use any numbers aher you initialize the variables!<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">A = 1;\r\nB = 500;\r\nC = 5;\r\nD = 2;\r\nE = 10;\r\nF = 3;\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.set_throttle((C * C) * F)\t# 5 * 5 * 3\t= 75; set throttle power t 75%\r\ndrone.move(A * D);\t        # 1 * 2 = 2 ; move for 2 seconds\r\ndrone.land()drone.close()\r\n\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 7 What are variables? [vc_column_text]Variables are important in all programming languages and in most programs! Just like in math, variables in programming are placeholders and data storage for&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-python-variables\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1760,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":808,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1737,1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,2164,2123,2122,2134,2117],"class_list":["post-1850","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-codrone","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-variables","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1850","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":3,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1850\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":3943,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1850\/revisions\/3943"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1760"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1850"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=1850"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=1850"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}